How to Reference LEAF Fonts in Your Code
Option 1: Use Pre-defined Font Families (Recommended)
Import the LEAF stylesheet and use the font family names directly:

CSS
/* In your CSS or SCSS */
font-family: 'PublicSans-Regular', sans-serif;
font-family: 'PublicSans-Medium', sans-serif;
font-family: 'PublicSans-Bold', sans-serif;
font-family: 'PublicSans-Light', sans-serif;
font-family: 'PublicSans-Thin', sans-serif;
font-family: 'Source Sans Pro Web', sans-serif;
font-family: 'Source Sans Pro Web-Bold', sans-serif;
font-family: 'fa-solid';  /* FontAwesome solid icons */
Example in HTML/CSS:

HTML
<style>
  .my-heading {
    font-family: 'PublicSans-Bold', sans-serif;
    font-size: 2rem;
  }
  
  .my-text {
    font-family: 'Source Sans Pro Web', sans-serif;
  }
</style>
Option 2: Import LEAF's Main Stylesheet
If you're working in a Vue/Webpack environment:

JavaScript
// In your JavaScript or Vue component
import '~/libs/css/leaf.css';  // or leaf.scss if using SASS

// Then use the fonts in your styles
export default {
  styles: `
    .custom-element {
      font-family: 'PublicSans-Medium', sans-serif;
    }
  `
}
Option 3: Reference Font Files Directly
If you need to define your own @font-face, you can reference the compiled fonts:

CSS
@font-face {
  font-family: 'MyCustomFont';
  src: url('/libs/css/fonts/PublicSans-Bold.woff2') format('woff2'),
       url('/libs/css/fonts/PublicSans-Bold.woff') format('woff');
}

.my-element {
  font-family: 'MyCustomFont', sans-serif;
}
Available Font Families
Public Sans (Regular weights)
PublicSans-Thin
PublicSans-Light
PublicSans-Regular
PublicSans-Medium
PublicSans-Bold
Source Sans Pro
Source Sans Pro Web (Regular)
Source Sans Pro Web-Bold (Bold)
Icons
fa-solid (FontAwesome solid icons)
Important Notes
Font files are in: docker/vue-app/src/common/assets/
Compiled output: libs/css/fonts/
Declarations: docker/vue-app/src/sass/leaf.scss
Always include a fallback font (e.g., sans-serif) in case the custom font doesn't load
Use woff2 format for modern browsers (better compression), with woff as a fallback for older browsers
